{
  "id": "episode-sibling-under-same-parent",
  "added": "2026-09-10",
  "mode": "recovery_pool",
  "why": "Recovery must reach a SIBLING under the submitted parent. The correct record here is another episode of the same series, and no similarity probe finds it: the submitted title is a season-and-episode compound that the sibling does not share, and there is no shared Alt ID. Only the STRUCTURAL pool -- children of the submitted parent -- reaches it (T11 rule 1).\n\nCaptured from a real mirror run on 2026-09-11 (`run_assessment.py --record-canon`), row 1787669628429059202 of the 2026-08 audit correction sheet, and cut with `cut_recovery_fixture.py`. The recall target was CONFIRMED by the operator, not taken from the engine's own proposal -- pinning the proposal would make the fixture assert that the engine agrees with itself.\n\nTHE INVARIANT IS RECALL, not a score. Under-recall produces a wrong OUTCOME rather than a worse-presented one: the reviewer sees nothing to compare, confirms No Match, and the record stays in the registry as a duplicate. A recall assertion also survives every retune, where a score would need re-pinning at each one.\n\nREPLAY SEMANTICS: the pool stands in for the REGISTRY and the search is re-run against it. Asserting that the recorded pool contains the answer would be true by construction and would pin nothing -- a search returning the whole registry would pass. Mutation-tested: disabling the structural pool returns 0 candidates from a 10-record registry and fails recall.\n\nThe pool holds 8 candidates AND 2 ancestors. The ancestors are not padding: without the parent chain the family gate cannot run, and the family gate decides whether a recovered candidate was eligible at all.\n\nSCOPE: pins that the sibling is REACHED. It says nothing about how the sibling scores once reached -- E00026 is the case where the pool succeeds and the score still falls short, and that is a separate lesson.",
